# BeNomad MSDK for Android

## Introduction
BeNomad's SDK for Android brings maps, address search, route planning and turn-by-turn guidance to your app, on
the phone and on Android Auto. It is split into modules: add only the ones your app needs.

### The modules

1. Error Manager: the SDK's error model, `Error` and `ErrorType`. Most modules report their errors to their callbacks as an `Error`. Mandatory, together with Core.

2. Core: required by almost every other module. Initializing it takes a purchase UUID (license) provided by BeNomad. Mandatory.

3. Mapping: interactive maps, customizable with styles and layers.

4. MapServices: downloads, updates and loads additional map data. It works only with dedicated data configured on BeNomad's servers.

5. Geocoder: online address suggestions (autocomplete), and offline address search, search around a location (reverse geocoding) and map-matching.

6. Vehicle Manager: the vehicle classes that the Planner and Navigation modules use. It can also fetch vehicle profiles from BeNomad's servers, for example electric vehicle profiles with which the Planner optimizes routes. Fetching them takes BeMap credentials, provided by BeNomad.

7. Traffic Manager: real-time traffic information.

8. Planner: route calculation, including routes for electric vehicles, which take EV profiles from the Vehicle Manager.

9. GPS Manager: the GPS sources of the Navigation module.

10. Navigation: tracking and guidance, including electric-vehicle guidance along the optimized routes of the Planner module.

11. Permissions Manager: requests the Android runtime permissions your app needs. You choose which ones (see its module page).

12. Android Auto: the building blocks of an Android Auto navigation app made with the SDK.

### Dependencies between the modules

| Module | Depends on |
|---|---|
| Error Manager | none |
| Core | Error Manager |
| Mapping | Core, Error Manager, Geocoder, Vehicle Manager |
| MapServices | Core, Error Manager |
| Geocoder | Core, Error Manager, Vehicle Manager |
| Vehicle Manager | Core, Error Manager |
| Traffic Manager | Core, Geocoder |
| Planner | Core, Error Manager, Geocoder, Traffic Manager, Vehicle Manager |
| GPS Manager | Core, Error Manager, Planner |
| Navigation | Core, Error Manager, Geocoder, GPS Manager, Mapping, Planner, Traffic Manager, Vehicle Manager |
| Permissions Manager | none |
| Android Auto | Core, Error Manager, Mapping, Navigation, Planner |

Gradle downloads the modules a module depends on, so you declare only the ones you use. Some of them are internal
(`settings-manager`, `bemap-api`, `http`, `audio-manager`). `audio-manager` holds `AudioType`, the type of the `type`
parameter of `InstructionsListener.onNewVocalInstruction`, in the Navigation module.

## Integration
The modules are Maven artifacts, on BeNomad's Nexus repository. You need its URL and credentials, provided by
BeNomad: for any question, write to msdk@benomad.com.

Steps 2 to 4 show each Gradle snippet in Groovy, then in the Kotlin DSL.

### 1. Store your Nexus credentials
In the `local.properties` file of your Android project:
```properties
NEXUS_URL=the-provided-url
NEXUS_USERNAME=the-provided-username
NEXUS_PASSWORD=the-provided-password
```
Don't commit `local.properties`: it holds your credentials.

### 2. Read them in your settings file
In `settings.gradle`, before `dependencyResolutionManagement`:
```groovy
Properties properties = new Properties()
if (file('local.properties').canRead()) {
    properties.load(file("local.properties").newDataInputStream())
}
```
In `settings.gradle.kts`:
```kotlin
val localProperties = java.util.Properties()
val localPropertiesFile = file("local.properties")
if (localPropertiesFile.canRead()) {
    localPropertiesFile.inputStream().use { localProperties.load(it) }
}
```

### 3. Declare BeNomad's Maven repository
In the same file, add the repository to `repositories`, in `dependencyResolutionManagement`:
```groovy
dependencyResolutionManagement {
    repositoriesMode.set(RepositoriesMode.FAIL_ON_PROJECT_REPOS)
    repositories {
        google()
        mavenCentral()
        maven {
            // HTTPS URL provided by BeNomad
            url = properties.getProperty('NEXUS_URL')
            credentials {
                username = properties.getProperty('NEXUS_USERNAME')
                password = properties.getProperty('NEXUS_PASSWORD')
            }
            // Only BeNomad's artifacts are resolved from this repository.
            content {
                includeGroup('com.benomad.sdk')
            }
        }
    }
}
```
In `settings.gradle.kts`:
```kotlin
dependencyResolutionManagement {
    repositoriesMode.set(RepositoriesMode.FAIL_ON_PROJECT_REPOS)
    repositories {
        google()
        mavenCentral()
        maven {
            // HTTPS URL provided by BeNomad
            url = uri(localProperties.getProperty("NEXUS_URL"))
            credentials {
                username = localProperties.getProperty("NEXUS_USERNAME")
                password = localProperties.getProperty("NEXUS_PASSWORD")
            }
            // Only BeNomad's artifacts are resolved from this repository.
            content {
                includeGroup("com.benomad.sdk")
            }
        }
    }
}
```

### 4. Configure your app module
Your app module needs:
- `compileSdk` 36 or higher, and the Android Gradle Plugin 8.9.1 or higher: the AndroidX libraries the SDK uses
  require them;
- `minSdk` 24 or higher;
- core library desugaring, with `desugar_jdk_libs` 2.1.5 or higher;
- a Kotlin version that reads the metadata of Kotlin 2.4, the version the modules are compiled with;
- the `INTERNET` permission in its manifest: the SDK's manifests don't declare it, and Core needs it to activate the
  license.

```groovy
android {
    compileSdk = 36 // or higher
    defaultConfig {
        minSdk = 24 // or higher
    }
    compileOptions {
        coreLibraryDesugaringEnabled = true
    }
}
dependencies {
    coreLibraryDesugaring 'com.android.tools:desugar_jdk_libs:2.1.5'
}
```
In `build.gradle.kts`:
```kotlin
android {
    compileSdk = 36 // or higher
    defaultConfig {
        minSdk = 24 // or higher
    }
    compileOptions {
        isCoreLibraryDesugaringEnabled = true
    }
}
dependencies {
    coreLibraryDesugaring("com.android.tools:desugar_jdk_libs:2.1.5")
}
```

### 5. Add the modules
In your app module's `build.gradle`, add Error Manager and Core, then the modules your app uses:
```groovy
dependencies {
    def msdk_version = '<version provided by BeNomad, for example 2.10.0.1>'

    // Mandatory
    implementation("com.benomad.sdk:error-manager:$msdk_version")
    implementation("com.benomad.sdk:core:$msdk_version")

    // The modules your app uses
    implementation("com.benomad.sdk:permissions-manager:$msdk_version")
    implementation("com.benomad.sdk:maps:$msdk_version")
    implementation("com.benomad.sdk:mapservices:$msdk_version")
    implementation("com.benomad.sdk:geocoder:$msdk_version")
    implementation("com.benomad.sdk:gps-manager:$msdk_version")
    implementation("com.benomad.sdk:vehicle-manager:$msdk_version")
    implementation("com.benomad.sdk:traffic-manager:$msdk_version")
    implementation("com.benomad.sdk:planner:$msdk_version")
    implementation("com.benomad.sdk:navigation:$msdk_version")
    implementation("com.benomad.sdk:android-auto:$msdk_version")
}
```
In `build.gradle.kts`, declare the version with `val msdk_version = "…"` instead of `def`.

Also declare every module, or library, whose types your own code uses, even when another module already brings it
in. For example:
- overriding `InstructionsListener.onNewVocalInstruction` takes `com.benomad.sdk:audio-manager`, for the type of its
  `AudioType` parameter;
- subclassing `MSDKSession` (Android Auto) takes `androidx.car.app:app`.
